WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.196
This is Lecture 10, part 4.

00:00:02.196 --> 00:00:05.074
We'll finish linked lists by sorting one,

00:00:05.074 --> 00:00:10.340
and then meet our last data structure, which isn't a line at all: the tree.

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:15.077
We'll learn the words for the parts of a tree, store one in Python,

00:00:15.077 --> 00:00:19.530
and walk it in 3 different orders, each a 3 line recursive function.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.148
Bubble sort is a simple sorting algorithm.

00:00:23.148 --> 00:00:29.860
Go through the list, compare every element with the next one, and swap them if the first is larger.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:33.661
Here is one pass on 5, 1, 4, 2.

00:00:33.661 --> 00:00:36.444
5 is bigger than 1: swap.

00:00:36.444 --> 00:00:39.227
5 is bigger than 4: swap.

00:00:39.227 --> 00:00:42.010
5 is bigger than 2: swap.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:47.320
After the pass, the largest element has bubbled up to the end.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.480
3 swaps, and 5 is in place.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:53.694
So the next pass can stop one element earlier.

00:00:53.694 --> 00:00:58.880
Keep going, one shorter each time, until only one element is left.

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:03.498
In the code, the variable n counts the elements that aren't sorted yet.

00:01:03.498 --> 00:01:07.706
Each pass compares neighbours with a for loop, swaps them in one line,

00:01:07.706 --> 00:01:09.870
and then the count goes down by one.

00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:14.973
Bubble sort only ever compares an element with the next one.

00:01:14.973 --> 00:01:17.987
So it works on a linked list just as well.

00:01:18.587 --> 00:01:24.405
Instead of an index, keep a reference called node, and hop to node.next.

00:01:24.405 --> 00:01:30.547
Instead of moving nodes, swap the elements stored in two neighbouring nodes.

00:01:30.897 --> 00:01:32.631
The arrows never change.

00:01:32.631 --> 00:01:35.377
Only the values inside the boxes move.

00:01:35.727 --> 00:01:44.116
The list from the slides, 9, 8, 6, 10, 45, 67, 21, 1, comes out sorted,

00:01:44.116 --> 00:01:46.007
from 1 up to 67.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.727
Lists, stacks and queues are linear.

00:01:49.727 --> 00:01:54.360
Every element has at most one before it and one after it.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:57.520
A tree stores elements hierarchically.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.844
Every element except the top one has one parent, and 0 or more children.

00:02:02.844 --> 00:02:06.910
The top element is the root, and it's drawn at the top.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.735
The structure of a company is a tree.

00:02:09.735 --> 00:02:11.674
Electronics R Us is the root.

00:02:11.674 --> 00:02:15.820
Its children are R and D, Sales, Purchasing and Manufacturing.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:19.009
Nodes with the same parent are siblings.

00:02:19.009 --> 00:02:21.421
A node with no children is a leaf.

00:02:21.421 --> 00:02:24.330
A node with children is an internal node.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:30.542
The depth of a node is the number of steps down from the root.

00:02:30.542 --> 00:02:32.244
Sales has depth 1.

00:02:32.244 --> 00:02:34.040
Canada has depth 3.

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:39.107
A node together with everything below it is a subtree,

00:02:39.107 --> 00:02:42.077
and that subtree is itself a tree.

00:02:42.077 --> 00:02:46.794
So a tree is a root, together with some smaller trees.

00:02:46.794 --> 00:02:51.510
That's a recursive definition, just like in Lecture 6.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:58.910
A tree is ordered if the children have a meaningful order, left to right, like the chapters of a book.

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:05.910
A binary tree is an ordered tree where every node has at most 2 children: a left child and a right child.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:11.820
In Python, a general tree node stores its element and a list of children.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:16.926
Printing a tree as an indented outline is our first tree algorithm.

00:03:16.926 --> 00:03:21.610
Print the node, then print each child's subtree, one level deeper.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:26.840
The indentation is the depth, just like the traced factorial in Lecture 6.

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:30.819
But outline makes one recursive call for each child.

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:36.470
That's multiple recursion, and its recursion tree is the very tree being printed.

00:03:37.267 --> 00:03:41.236
An arithmetic expression can be stored as a binary tree.

00:03:41.236 --> 00:03:45.560
The leaves are numbers, and the internal nodes are operators.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:49.387
Each operator applies to the values of its 2 subtrees.

00:03:49.737 --> 00:03:52.188
This is the slides' expression tree.

00:03:52.188 --> 00:03:53.413
The root is minus.

00:03:53.413 --> 00:03:55.047
On the left, a division.

00:03:55.047 --> 00:03:56.817
On the right, an addition.

00:03:57.167 --> 00:04:03.572
In Python, a binary node has an element, a left subtree and a right subtree.

00:04:03.572 --> 00:04:05.173
Either can be None.

00:04:05.173 --> 00:04:08.207
A node is a leaf when both are None.

00:04:09.001 --> 00:04:12.525
A traversal visits every node of a tree once.

00:04:12.525 --> 00:04:16.128
For a binary tree, there are 3 natural orders.

00:04:16.128 --> 00:04:20.201
They differ only in when the node itself is visited.

00:04:20.551 --> 00:04:25.631
Preorder visits the node first, then its left subtree, then its right.

00:04:25.981 --> 00:04:30.581
Inorder visits the left subtree, then the node, then the right.

00:04:30.931 --> 00:04:36.891
Postorder visits the left subtree, then the right, then the node last.

00:04:37.241 --> 00:04:38.504
Each is 3 lines.

00:04:38.504 --> 00:04:43.322
The base case is the empty tree, None, which has no elements.

00:04:43.322 --> 00:04:47.349
And the leap of faith from Lecture 6 does the rest:

00:04:47.349 --> 00:04:52.641
trust each recursive call to return its subtree in the right order.

00:04:53.434 --> 00:04:55.422
Pause the video and predict.

00:04:55.422 --> 00:05:00.464
What are the first 4 elements of each traversal of our expression tree?

00:05:00.464 --> 00:05:03.234
And which traversal ends with the root?

00:05:03.784 --> 00:05:11.504
Preorder starts at the root, and goes left: minus, divide, times, plus.

00:05:11.854 --> 00:05:16.968
Inorder starts at the leftmost leaf: 3, plus, 1, times.

00:05:16.968 --> 00:05:22.734
It reads like the expression we'd write, but without brackets.

00:05:23.084 --> 00:05:29.004
Postorder also starts with the leftmost leaf: 3, 1, plus, 3.

00:05:29.354 --> 00:05:33.899
And postorder is the one that ends with the root, minus,

00:05:33.899 --> 00:05:37.794
because it visits every node after its subtrees.

00:05:38.394 --> 00:05:43.566
Inorder lost the brackets, so it no longer says which operation comes first.

00:05:43.566 --> 00:05:48.874
Put a pair of brackets around each subtree, and the expression is exact again.

00:05:49.667 --> 00:05:56.842
To evaluate the expression, an operator can only be applied after both of its subtrees have been evaluated.

00:05:56.842 --> 00:05:58.787
That's a postorder traversal.

00:05:59.137 --> 00:06:01.092
A leaf returns its number.

00:06:01.092 --> 00:06:06.657
Otherwise, evaluate the left, evaluate the right, then apply the operator.

00:06:07.007 --> 00:06:12.842
On the left: 3 plus 1 is 4, times 3 is 12.

00:06:12.842 --> 00:06:16.732
9 minus 5 is 4, plus 2 is 6.

00:06:16.732 --> 00:06:19.927
12 divided by 6 is 2.0.

00:06:20.277 --> 00:06:24.017
On the right: 7 minus 4 is 3.

00:06:24.017 --> 00:06:27.757
3 times 3 is 9, plus 6 is 15.

00:06:28.107 --> 00:06:32.436
At the root: 2.0 minus 15 is minus 13.0.

00:06:32.436 --> 00:06:39.147
It's a float, because division always gives a float in Python.

00:06:39.934 --> 00:06:40.583
One trap.

00:06:40.583 --> 00:06:42.962
A leaf's left and right are None.

00:06:42.962 --> 00:06:51.614
A traversal that reaches for node.left.element without checking crashes at the very first leaf, with an attribute error.

00:06:51.964 --> 00:06:55.865
Write the base case first, as in Lecture 6:

00:06:55.865 --> 00:06:59.404
if node is None, return the empty list.

00:07:00.201 --> 00:07:02.551
The slides end with 2 more trees.

00:07:02.551 --> 00:07:05.186
The paths through a maze form a tree.

00:07:05.186 --> 00:07:10.384
The start is the root, and every fork has one child for each way forward.

00:07:10.384 --> 00:07:13.161
The leaves are dead ends, and the exit.

00:07:13.511 --> 00:07:16.377
The positions of a game form a tree too.

00:07:16.377 --> 00:07:23.111
The current position is the root, and its children are the positions after each possible move.

00:07:23.461 --> 00:07:27.781
Finding the way out, or the best move, means searching the tree.

00:07:27.781 --> 00:07:30.953
You'll meet these searches again in AI courses,

00:07:30.953 --> 00:07:34.261
where they're the start of game playing programs.

00:07:35.034 --> 00:07:36.314
Let's sum up.

00:07:36.664 --> 00:07:39.730
Bubble sort only compares neighbours,

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:43.624
so on a linked list it hops and swaps elements.

00:07:43.974 --> 00:07:51.734
A tree is a root plus smaller trees: parent, child, leaf, subtree and depth.

00:07:52.084 --> 00:07:57.651
Preorder, inorder and postorder differ only in when the node is visited.

00:07:57.651 --> 00:08:01.284
And evaluating an expression tree is postorder.

00:08:01.634 --> 00:08:02.909
Here's one to try.

00:08:02.909 --> 00:08:06.735
The height of a tree is the largest depth of any node.

00:08:06.735 --> 00:08:10.419
Write a recursive height function for a binary tree.

00:08:10.419 --> 00:08:13.394
What is the height of our expression tree?

00:08:13.944 --> 00:08:15.763
That's the end of Lecture 10.

00:08:15.763 --> 00:08:19.212
In Lecture 11, we'll look inside large language models,

00:08:19.212 --> 00:08:21.344
the programs behind AI assistants.

